    "narrative": "For today\u2019s slate, **Corbin Carroll** leads the charge with an elite 80.29 score, benefiting from a prime matchup against Michael Lorenzen. His combination of favorable park factors and dynamic platoon advantage makes him our most potent asset for high-ceiling production. Meanwhile, **Brice Turang** and **James Wood** warrant high-leverage attention; **Brice Turang** earns a 74.07 score against Roki Sasaki thanks to a massive 19% boost from the pitcher\u2019s skill profile and an optimal #2 spot in the order, while **James Wood** rides a strong 68.19 mark fueled by a premium leadoff position and a solid 0.380 xwOBA. We are also monitoring **Josh Naylor**, whose 69.12 score is buoyed by a favorable platoon split against Stephen Kolek, making him a cornerstone for RBI production today.\n\nThe most difficult decision on the bench was leaving **Kyle Karros** out of the lineup. With a score of 51.32, he was the primary candidate for the utility spot, but he fell just short of **Vinnie Pasquantino**, whose 57.72 score was heavily propped up by a significant 14% boost against George Kirby. While **Vinnie Pasquantino** faces a tougher arm, the platoon advantage is too valuable to ignore in our current build. Elsewhere, we are proceeding with caution regarding our \"Lineup Pending\" designations; since all starters are currently projected based on historical tendencies, any late-breaking lineup changes\u2014particularly for **Zach McKinstry**\u2014could force an immediate pivot. We remain concerned about the risk surrounding **Cole Young**, whose score of 45.87 is borderline, and we are tracking the status of **Mickey Moniak** and **Jose Caballero**, who remain sidelined due to injury and a rainout, respectively.",
